__registerContent("newspaper/polity/2026-08-28/lok-sabha-543", {"notes":{"news-2026-08-28-lok-sabha-543":{"newsType":true,"date":"28 August 2026","paper":"Editorial (S.Y. Quraishi, former CEC)","subject":"Polity / GS-2 (Representation, Federalism, Delimitation)","summary":"With the freeze on Lok Sabha seats set to end after the first Census taken post-2026, there is speculation about enlarging the Lok Sabha through delimitation. Former Chief Election Commissioner S.Y. Quraishi argues that the House should remain at 543. Article 82 requires readjustment of seats after each Census, but the 42nd (1976) and 84th (2001) Amendments froze the state-wise allocation (based on the 1971 Census) to protect states that succeeded in population control — a federal compact. A population-based enlargement would shift power sharply to populous northern states (e.g. UP) at the expense of the south (e.g. Tamil Nadu), widening the absolute gap even if proportionality is maintained. A bigger House also weakens deliberation. Quraishi contends delimitation, federal fairness and women's reservation can all be achieved WITHOUT enlarging the Lok Sabha — by internal redistribution within states using updated figures, and by expanding State Assemblies (Vidhan Sabhas).","keyPoints":[{"heading":"Constitutional Framework","points":["Article 81: composition of the Lok Sabha (max 550 elected — 530 from states, 20 from UTs)","Article 82: readjustment (delimitation) of seats after every Census 'so far as practicable' in proportion to population","42nd Amendment (1976): froze seat allocation based on the 1971 Census until 2000","84th Amendment (2001): extended the freeze to the first Census taken after 2026","87th Amendment (2003): allowed delimitation of constituencies within states on the basis of the 2001 Census (without changing each state's total seats)"]},{"heading":"Why 'Numbers Alone' Cannot Decide","points":["Representation is proportional to population only 'so far as practicable' — not pure arithmetic","Southern states that succeeded in family planning would be penalised by a population-based reallocation","Example: UP could rise from 80 to ~120 seats and Tamil Nadu from 39 to ~59 — proportion preserved but the absolute power gap widens","The 1971 freeze embodied a federal compact: states stabilising population must not lose political voice"]},{"heading":"Bigger House = Weaker Deliberation","points":["More MPs mean less time for each to speak and scrutinise","Larger constituencies (approaching US congressional-district size) weaken the representative-citizen link","Quality of deliberation — Parliament's core function — would decline"]},{"heading":"The Author's Prescription — Preserve 543","points":["Retain 543 seats; undertake internal redistribution of constituencies WITHIN each state using updated (2027) Census figures","Implement women's reservation within the existing structure","Expand State Legislative Assemblies (Vidhan Sabhas) instead, to absorb the demand for more representation","Delimitation and women's representation are necessary/overdue, but enlarging the Lok Sabha beyond 543 is not"]}],"importantTerms":[{"term":"Delimitation","explanation":"The redrawing of boundaries and readjustment of the number of seats of legislative constituencies to reflect population changes, done by a Delimitation Commission. Governed by Article 82; the last delimitation used the 2001 Census (87th Amendment)."},{"term":"Seat Freeze (1971 Census basis)","explanation":"The 42nd Amendment (1976) froze the allocation of Lok Sabha seats among states based on the 1971 Census; the 84th Amendment (2001) extended this freeze to the first Census after 2026 — protecting states that reduced their population growth."},{"term":"Women's Reservation (Nari Shakti Vandan Adhiniyam)","explanation":"The 106th Constitutional Amendment Act, 2023 provides one-third reservation for women in the Lok Sabha and State Assemblies. Its implementation is tied to a delimitation exercise following the next Census."},{"term":"Article 81 & 550 cap","explanation":"Article 81 caps the Lok Sabha at 550 elected members (530 from states + 20 from UTs). The current strength is 543 elected members."}],"mainsAnswerNotes":["The delimitation debate pits the democratic principle of 'one person, one vote, one value' against the federal principle of protecting states that achieved population stabilisation — a classic tension in Indian federalism.","A purely population-based enlargement would concentrate parliamentary power in a few northern states, potentially deepening north-south divides and undermining cooperative federalism.","Enlarging the House risks diluting deliberative quality; the author argues reforms (fair internal redistribution, women's reservation) are achievable within 543.","Alternatives that balance both principles: retain 543 with internal redistribution, strengthen State Assemblies, and consider whether representation quality — not just quantity — should guide reform.","The women's reservation (106th Amendment, 2023), being linked to delimitation, makes the timing and design of the exercise politically consequential."],"prelims":["Article 82: readjustment of seats after each Census; Article 81: composition/cap (max 550 elected) of Lok Sabha","42nd Amendment (1976) froze seats on 1971 Census basis till 2000; 84th Amendment (2001) extended to first Census after 2026","87th Amendment (2003): delimitation of constituencies on 2001 Census basis, without altering states' seat totals","Current Lok Sabha elected strength: 543","Women's Reservation = 106th Amendment Act, 2023 (Nari Shakti Vandan Adhiniyam), one-third seats, implemented after delimitation","S.Y. Quraishi is a former Chief Election Commissioner of India"]}},"quizzes":{"news-2026-08-28-lok-sabha-543":{"title":"Why 543 Should Remain 543 (Delimitation)","questions":[{"q":"Which Article of the Constitution provides for the readjustment (delimitation) of seats after every Census?","options":["Article 80","Article 81","Article 82","Article 84"],"correct":2,"explain":"Article 82 provides that upon completion of each Census, the allocation of seats and division into constituencies shall be readjusted 'so far as practicable' in proportion to population."},{"q":"The allocation of Lok Sabha seats among the states is currently frozen on the basis of which Census?","options":["1951 Census","1971 Census","1991 Census","2001 Census"],"correct":1,"explain":"The seat allocation is frozen on the basis of the 1971 Census, first by the 42nd Amendment and later extended by the 84th Amendment."},{"q":["Consider the following statements:","I. The 42nd Amendment (1976) froze the state-wise allocation of Lok Sabha seats until 2000.","II. The 84th Amendment (2001) extended this freeze to the first Census taken after 2026.","Which of the statements given above is/are correct?"],"options":["I only","II only","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"correct":2,"explain":"Both are correct — the 42nd Amendment froze allocation till 2000 and the 84th Amendment extended it to the first Census after 2026."},{"q":"What is the maximum strength of elected members of the Lok Sabha as per Article 81?","options":["525","543","550","552"],"correct":2,"explain":"Article 81 caps the Lok Sabha at 550 elected members (530 from states + 20 from UTs). The current elected strength is 543."},{"q":"The phrase used in Article 82 regarding proportionality of representation to population is:","options":["'in exact proportion'","'so far as practicable'","'strictly according to numbers'","'as the President directs'"],"correct":1,"explain":"Article 82 requires representation to be proportional to population only 'so far as practicable' — the author stresses that numbers alone cannot decide."},{"q":"Which amendment permitted the delimitation of constituencies within states on the basis of the 2001 Census, without altering each state's total number of seats?","options":["42nd Amendment","84th Amendment","87th Amendment","91st Amendment"],"correct":2,"explain":"The 87th Amendment (2003) allowed readjustment of constituencies within states based on the 2001 Census population, keeping the state-wise seat totals frozen."},{"q":"Assertion (A): The freeze on Lok Sabha seats was intended to protect certain states.\nReason (R): States that successfully controlled their population growth could otherwise lose political representation relative to states with high growth.\nWhich one of the following is correct?","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"correct":0,"explain":"Both are true and R explains A — the freeze was a federal compact to ensure states stabilising their population would not be penalised with fewer seats."},{"q":"According to the article, a population-based enlargement of the Lok Sabha would primarily benefit:","options":["Southern states like Tamil Nadu and Kerala","Populous northern states like Uttar Pradesh and Bihar","Union Territories","North-eastern states"],"correct":1,"explain":"Populous northern states (e.g. UP, Bihar) would gain seats, while southern states that curbed population growth would see their relative weight fall."},{"q":"The Women's Reservation Act (Nari Shakti Vandan Adhiniyam) is which Constitutional Amendment?","options":["101st Amendment","103rd Amendment","105th Amendment","106th Amendment"],"correct":3,"explain":"The 106th Constitutional Amendment Act, 2023 provides one-third reservation for women in the Lok Sabha and State Assemblies."},{"q":["Consider the following statements about women's reservation:","I. It provides one-third reservation in the Lok Sabha and State Legislative Assemblies.","II. Its implementation is linked to a delimitation exercise following a Census.","Which of the statements given above is/are correct?"],"options":["I only","II only","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"correct":2,"explain":"Both are correct — the 106th Amendment provides one-third reservation and ties its rollout to delimitation after the next Census."},{"q":"The author S.Y. Quraishi previously held which constitutional office?","options":["Attorney General of India","Comptroller and Auditor General","Chief Election Commissioner","Chairman of the Finance Commission"],"correct":2,"explain":"S.Y. Quraishi is a former Chief Election Commissioner of India."},{"q":"Which of the following is the author's central recommendation?","options":["Double the size of the Lok Sabha to reflect population growth","Retain 543 seats, redistribute constituencies within states, and expand State Assemblies instead","Abolish delimitation altogether","Freeze seats permanently and end all Censuses"],"correct":1,"explain":"The author argues for keeping 543, undertaking internal redistribution within states using updated figures, implementing women's reservation within this structure, and expanding Vidhan Sabhas."},{"q":"The author argues that enlarging the Lok Sabha would harm which core parliamentary function?","options":["Tax collection","The quality of deliberation and debate","Judicial review","Election scheduling"],"correct":1,"explain":"A much larger House would give each MP less time to speak and enlarge constituencies, weakening the quality of deliberation — Parliament's most important function."},{"q":["Consider the following statements about delimitation in India:","I. It is carried out by a Delimitation Commission.","II. The last delimitation of constituencies was based on the 2001 Census.","III. Article 82 links delimitation to the Census.","How many of the statements are correct?"],"options":["Only one","Only two","All three","None"],"correct":2,"explain":"All three are correct — delimitation is done by a Delimitation Commission, the last exercise used the 2001 Census (87th Amendment), and Article 82 links it to the Census."},{"q":"In the article's illustration, Tamil Nadu's seats might rise from 39 to about 59 while UP rises from 80 to about 120. This example is used to show that:","options":["Proportionality would be violated","Even if proportionality is preserved, the ABSOLUTE gap in seats between states widens","Tamil Nadu would gain more than UP","The total House size would remain unchanged"],"correct":1,"explain":"The example shows that population-based enlargement keeps proportions similar but widens the absolute seat gap, shifting the balance of power towards larger states."},{"q":"The Lok Sabha and Rajya Sabha were adjourned sine die and the session prorogued around which date mentioned in the article?","options":["13 August 2026","15 August 2026","1 September 2026","26 January 2026"],"correct":0,"explain":"The article notes both Houses were adjourned sine die on 13 August 2026, with the monsoon session subsequently prorogued."},{"q":["Consider the following statements regarding the '1971 freeze':","I. It reflects a federal compact.","II. It aimed to prevent states that stabilised population from losing political voice.","III. It permanently abolished delimitation.","Which of the statements given above are correct?"],"options":["I and II only","II and III only","I and III only","I, II and III"],"correct":0,"explain":"Statements I and II are correct. III is wrong — the freeze only paused seat REALLOCATION among states; delimitation of constituencies within states continued (e.g. based on 2001 Census)."},{"q":"The author suggests that the demand for more representation could be accommodated by expanding which body instead of the Lok Sabha?","options":["The Rajya Sabha","State Legislative Assemblies (Vidhan Sabhas)","The Panchayats only","The Union Council of Ministers"],"correct":1,"explain":"The author proposes expanding State Legislative Assemblies (Vidhan Sabhas) to absorb the demand for greater representation, leaving the Lok Sabha at 543."},{"q":"Which principle BEST captures the tension in the delimitation debate as framed by the article?","options":["Separation of powers vs judicial review","'One person, one vote, one value' (democratic equality) vs protecting states that controlled population (federal fairness)","Fundamental rights vs directive principles","Centre's power vs the judiciary's power"],"correct":1,"explain":"The debate pits the democratic principle of equal value of each vote against the federal principle of not penalising states that succeeded in population stabilisation."},{"q":"Which Census is expected to provide the basis for the next delimitation/redistribution referred to in the article?","options":["The 2011 Census","The 2021 Census","The Census taken after 2026 (e.g. 2027)","No Census is needed"],"correct":2,"explain":"The freeze ends after the first Census taken post-2026; the article refers to the 2027 Census providing the basis for reforming constituencies within each state."}]}}});
